{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"In 1976 energy policy analyst Amory Lovins coined the term soft energy path to describe an alternative future where energy efficiency and appropriate renewable energy sources steadily replace a centralized energy system based on fossil and nuclear fuels.","title":"Soft energy path"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Amory Bloch Lovins (born November 13, 1947) is an American physicist, environmental scientist, writer, and Chairman/Chief Scientist of the Rocky Mountain Institute.","title":"Amory Lovins"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He has worked in the field of energy policy and related areas for four decades.","title":"Amory Lovins"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He was named by "Time" magazine one of the World's 100 most influential people in 2009.","title":"Amory Lovins"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The concept of the soft path was first used for energy resource management and was developed by Amory Lovins shortly after the shock of the 1973 energy crisis in the United States.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This concept has now been refined and applied to water, most notably by water expert Peter Gleick and David Brooks.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The soft path is often framed as a more integrated and effective alternative to supply-side water resource management.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Supply-side water management focuses on meeting demands for water through centralized, large-scale physical infrastructure, and centralized water management systems.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In the 20th century, this approach focused on constructing bigger dams and drilling deeper wells to access more water to meet projected demands of consumers.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" More recently, a focus on demand-side management has emerged in regions where water supply is increasingly constrained (see, for example, Peak water), and it focuses on managing demand and making current practices more efficient.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The soft path integrates both supply and demand concepts but in a broader context by recognizing that water is a means to satisfy demands for goods and services and asking how much water, of what qualities, is actually required to satisfy those demands efficiently and sustainably.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Soft path water planning also requires broader institutional approaches to water management including the application of smart economics, the potential for distributed rather than centralized water systems, and more democratic participation in water policy decisions.","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Others have described the soft path as "unleashing the full potential of demand-side management."","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":9,"text":",","title":"Soft water path"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Negawatt power is a theoretical unit of power representing an amount of electrical power (measured in watts) saved.","title":"Negawatt power"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The energy saved is a direct result of energy conservation or increased energy efficiency.","title":"Negawatt power"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The term was coined by the chief scientist of the Rocky Mountain Institute and environmentalist Amory Lovins in 1985, within the article, "Saving Gigabucks with Negawatts," where he argued that utility customers don’t want kilowatt-hours of electricity; they want energy services such as hot showers, cold beer, lit rooms, and spinning shafts, which can come more cheaply if electricity is used more efficiently.","title":"Negawatt power"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Lovins felt an international behavioral change was necessary in order to decrease countries' dependence on excessive amounts of energy.","title":"Negawatt power"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The concept of a negawatt could influence a behavioral change in consumers by encouraging them to think about the energy that they spend.","title":"Negawatt power"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Jacob "Jack" Goldman (Brooklyn, New York, July 18, 1921 – Westport, Connecticut, December 20, 2011) was an American physicist and former chief scientist of Xerox Corporation.","title":"Jack Goldman"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was also a faculty member at Carnegie Tech and directed the Ford Scientific Laboratory.","title":"Jack Goldman"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is especially notable for hiring physicist Dr. George Pake to create the Xerox Palo Alto Research Center, which produced many seminal ideas in modern computing.","title":"Jack Goldman"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"John Mace Grunsfeld (born October 10, 1958) is an American physicist and a former NASA astronaut.","title":"John M. Grunsfeld"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He is a veteran of five Space Shuttle flights and has served as NASA Chief Scientist.","title":"John M. Grunsfeld"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" His academic background includes research in high energy astrophysics, cosmic ray physics and the emerging field of exoplanet studies with specific interest in future astronomical instrumentation.","title":"John M. Grunsfeld"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" After retiring from NASA in 2009, he served as the Deputy Director of the Space Telescope Science Institute in Baltimore, Maryland.","title":"John M. Grunsfeld"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In January 2012, he returned to NASA and served as associate administrator of NASA's Science Mission Directorate (SMD).","title":"John M. Grunsfeld"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Grunsfeld announced his retirement from NASA in April 2016.","title":"John M. Grunsfeld"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Rocky Mountain Institute
