{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The moose (North America) or elk (Eurasia), "Alces alces", is the largest extant species in the deer family.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Moose are distinguished by the broad, flat (or "palmate") antlers of the males; other members of the family have antlers with a dendritic ("twig-like") configuration.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Moose typically inhabit boreal forests and temperate broadleaf and mixed forests of the Northern Hemisphere in temperate to subarctic climates. Hunting and other human activities have caused a reduction in the size of the moose's range over time.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Moose have been reintroduced to some of their former habitats.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Currently, most moose are found in Canada, Alaska, New England, Fennoscandia, Baltic States, and Russia.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" Their diet consists of both terrestrial and aquatic vegetation.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The most common moose predators are the gray wolf along with bears and humans. Unlike most other deer species, moose are solitary animals and do not form herds.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" Although generally slow-moving and sedentary, moose can become aggressive and move quickly if angered or startled.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":8,"text":" Their mating season in the autumn features energetic fights between males competing for a female.","title":"Moose"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Forests of Mara and Mondrem were adjacent medieval forests in Cheshire, England, which in the 11th century extended to over 60 sqmi , stretching from the Mersey in the north almost to Nantwich in the south, and from the Gowy in the west to the Weaver in the east.","title":"Forests of Mara and Mondrem"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Mara and Mondrem were a hunting forest of the Norman Earls of Chester, established soon after 1071 by the first earl, Hugh d'Avranches.","title":"Forests of Mara and Mondrem"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" They might earlier have been an Anglo-Saxon hunting forest.","title":"Forests of Mara and Mondrem"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Game included wild boar, and red, fallow and roe deer.","title":"Forests of Mara and Mondrem"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Sri Lankan sambar deer ("Rusa unicolor unicolor") is a subspecies of sambar deer that lives in Sri Lanka.","title":"Sri Lankan sambar deer"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" This subspecies is one of the largest sambar deer species with the largest antlers both in size and in body proportions.","title":"Sri Lankan sambar deer"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Large males weight up to 270–280 kg.","title":"Sri Lankan sambar deer"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Sri Lankan sambar live in lowland dry forests and mountain forests.","title":"Sri Lankan sambar deer"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Large herds of sambar deer roam the Horton Plains National Park, where it is the most common large mammal.","title":"Sri Lankan sambar deer"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Visayan spotted deer ("Rusa alfredi"), also known as the Philippine spotted deer, is a nocturnal and endangered species of deer located primarily in the rainforests of the Visayan islands of Panay and Negros though it once roamed other islands such as Cebu, Guimaras, Leyte, Masbate, and Samar.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It is one of three endemic deer species in the Philippines, although it was not recognized as a separate species until 1983.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" An estimated 2,500 mature individuals survived worldwide as of 1996, according to the IUCN, although it is uncertain of how many of them still survive in the wild.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The diet of the deer, which consists of a variety of different types of grasses, leaves, and buds within the forest, is the primary indicator of its habitat.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Since 1991 the range of the species has severely decreased and is now almost co-extensive with that of the Visayan warty pig.","title":"Visayan spotted deer"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The red deer ("Cervus elaphus") is one of the largest deer species.","title":"Red deer"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The red deer inhabits most of Europe, the Caucasus Mountains region, Asia Minor, Iran, parts of western Asia, and central Asia.","title":"Red deer"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" It also inhabits the Atlas Mountains region between Morocco and Tunisia in northwestern Africa, being the only species of deer to inhabit Africa.","title":"Red deer"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Red deer have been introduced to other areas, including Australia, New Zealand, United States, Canada, Peru, Uruguay, Chile and Argentina.","title":"Red deer"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" In many parts of the world, the meat (venison) from red deer is used as a food source.","title":"Red deer"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Dafeng () is a coastal district under the administration of Yancheng, Jiangsu province, China.","title":"Dafeng District"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Located on the Jiangsu North Plain with a coastline of 112 km , Dafeng was historically one of the largest salt-making areas in China and now is famed for its well preserved eco-system and numerous national conservation parks.","title":"Dafeng District"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The district has the largest national nature reserve for a rare deer species, Père David's Deer or Milu (麋鹿 ) in Chinese.","title":"Dafeng District"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It borders the prefecture-level city of Taizhou to the southwest.","title":"Dafeng District"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Red deer also occur throughout most of Europe, the Caucasus, Asia Minor, Iran, western and central Asia, and have been introduced to Australia, New Zealand, the United States, Canada, Peru, Uruguay,